cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset or the accompanying thesis, please cite as below."
title: "In Tlilli, In Tlapalli: A Codicological Survey of the Codex Borgia and Florentine Codex"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Stevenson"
    given-names: "Michael"
version: 2.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-29"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21609725
repository-code: "https://github.com/mstevensonart/florentine-codex-borgia-codicology"
url: "https://mstevensonart.github.io/florentine-codex-borgia-codicology/"
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >
  A codicological survey documenting watermarks, paper stock, tool evidence,
  and scribal corrections across the Codex Borgia (Borgiana Messicana 1,
  Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana) and the Florentine Codex (Codice Fiorentino,
  Medici Palatino 218-220, Biblioteca Mediceana Laurenziana), building toward
  an argument for artisanal continuity from a pre-Conquest screenfold to a
  colonial manuscript. Grew out of the author's M.A. thesis (Art History,
  Arizona State University, 2002; chair: Emily Umberger).
keywords:
  - codicology
  - Codex Borgia
  - Florentine Codex
  - Mesoamerican manuscripts
  - manuscript studies
  - art history
